## Problem4('17')
## KEYWORDS('calculus','derivative','linear approximation','tangent line','approximation')

## Textbook tags
## HHChapter1('Short-Cuts to Differentiation')
## HHChapter2('Short-Cuts to Differentiation')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "PGlateximage.pl",
  "PGML.pl",
  "parserPopUp.pl",
  "PGcourse.pl"
);
 
Context("Numeric");
Context()->strings->add( under=>{}, over=>{} );

$a = random(1,5,1);
$a2 = $a*2;
$m = random(2,5,1);
$b = random(1,5,1);
$c = $a + random(0.1,0.7,0.1);
$est = $m*$c - $b;

$graph = createLaTeXImage();
$graph->texPackages(['pgfplots']);
$graph->BEGIN_LATEX_IMAGE
\begin{tikzpicture}
	\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, grid=both, yticklabels={0}, xtick={1,2}, xticklabels={\($a\), \($a2\)}]
		\addplot[domain=0:1.39, thick]{e^(2*x^2-2*x)};
		\addplot[domain=-1:2, thick]{2*x-1};
	\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
END_LATEX_IMAGE

$overunder = DropDown(['under', 'over'], 0);

BEGIN_PGML
The figure below shows [`f(x)`] and its local linearization at
[`x=a`], [`y = [$m] x - [$b]`].

>> [!a graph of a concave up function with a line tangent to the curve when x=[$a]!]{$graph}{300} <<

a. What is the value of [`a`]?  
   [`a ={}`][_]{$a}{15}
a. What is the value of [`f(a)`]?  
   [`f(a) ={}`][_]{$m*$a - $b}{15}
a. Use the linearization to approximate the value of [`f([$c])`].  
   [`f([$c]) \approx{}`][_]{$est}{15}
a. Is the approximation an under- or overestimate?  
   [_]{$overunder}

END_PGML

$f0 = $m*$a - $b;
$f1 = $m*$c - $b;

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Since the line meets the curve at [`x=[$a]`], we have [`a=[$a]`].  Since the
point with [`x=[$a]`] lies on both the line and the curve, we have
[```f(a) = f([$a]) = [$m]([$a]) - [$b] = [$f0].```]
The approximation is an underestimate because the line lies under the
curve.  Since the linear function approximates [`f(x)`], we have
[```f([$c]) \approx [$m]([$c]) - [$b] = [$f1]```]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
